|description={{en|1=The Birth of Shah Rukh in Samarkand, Shiraz, Zafarnama 1436<br>

* Depiction of [[:en:Shah Rukh|Shah Rukh]]: "So while these two compositions pay homage to the aims of Timur's political ideology, it is hardly surprising to find that Timur's ultimate successor and the father of the patron of the whole Zafarnama project -text and pictures alike - Shahrukh, should be the subject of five compositions in Ibrahim-Sultan's Zafarnama. '''The birth of Shahrukh is a marvelous single page painting. A single-page painting in Seattle shows Saray-Mulk Khanum on the road to Marand with Shahrukh and Khalil-Sultan in the spring of 1387.''' The well-known Freer Gallery painting is actually only the right half of a double-page composition showing the triumphal entry into Samarqand made by Shahrukh in Dhu'I-Hijja 796 (September 1394), after Timur appointed him governor of that city. The last composition in the manuscript, also a double-page painting divided for many years - its left half, showing the vanguard of the army with drummers, trumpeters, and flag bearers, was one of the unidentified paintings exhibited in London in 1931! - and most recently, between Tehran and New York City, shows Shahrukh leading the Timurid army after the death of Timur in February 1405."in Sims, E. (1992). "Ibrahim-Sultan’s Illustrated Zafarnama of 1436 and its Impact in the Muslim East", p.136 in Timurid Art and Culture. Leiden, The Netherlands: Brill. [https://doi-org.wikipedialibrary.idm.oclc.org/10.1163/9789004662551_014] 

}}
|date=1436-12-31
|source=[https://thegreatgodpanisdead.substack.com/p/houstons-zafarnama]
